WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.101
We know the Republican

00:00:01.101 --> 00:00:02.669
presidential field will grow by three

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:03.236
this week.

00:00:03.236 --> 00:00:04.070
Mike Pence, Chris

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.138
Christie and Doug Burgum

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:06.873
all have announcements teed up

00:00:06.873 --> 00:00:08.675
that will get us to ten declared

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.810
GOP candidates Will that be it?

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:12.779
Or will there be more?

00:00:12.779 --> 00:00:14.814
Chris Sununu has been a maybe for months,

00:00:15.081 --> 00:00:16.082
a Donald Trump critic

00:00:16.082 --> 00:00:16.716
who is also

00:00:16.716 --> 00:00:18.451
the popular governor of the state

00:00:18.451 --> 00:00:19.619
with the first primary

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:21.221
on the Republican nominating calendar.

00:00:21.221 --> 00:00:22.188
Well, today,

00:00:22.188 --> 00:00:23.390
Governor Sununu is ready

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.125
to share his decision right here.

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.793
CNN's Dana Bash live in New Hampshire

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:27.961
with this exclusive interview.

00:00:27.961 --> 00:00:28.561
Dana,

00:00:31.064 --> 00:00:31.931
John, thank you.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:32.465
And, Governor,

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:33.967
thank you so much for inviting us here

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:36.069
to the Granite State for your decision.

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:36.936
Yes.

00:00:36.936 --> 00:00:38.571
So we've taken the last six months

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:39.305
to really kind of

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:40.640
look at things where everything is.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.574
And I've made the decision

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:43.243
not to run for president

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:45.078
on the Republican ticket in 20, 24.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:45.445
And obviously

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:46.713
a lot goes into that decision.

00:00:46.713 --> 00:00:48.515
But it's been quite an adventure.

00:00:48.515 --> 00:00:49.849
But not the end of the adventure

00:00:49.849 --> 00:00:51.551
by any means. Why?

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:52.419
Why won't you run?

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:53.086
Well,

00:00:53.086 --> 00:00:53.586
when you look at

00:00:53.586 --> 00:00:54.454
what's happening out there,

00:00:54.454 --> 00:00:56.790
you obviously have a very large field of

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:58.558
I think the former president Trump

00:00:58.558 --> 00:00:59.325
is doing much better

00:00:59.325 --> 00:01:00.860
in the polls than folks thought.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:01.861
And when I looked at

00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:03.229
where my voice can be,

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.065
how we make the Republican Party

00:01:05.065 --> 00:01:05.532
bigger,

00:01:05.532 --> 00:01:06.900
the responsibility that I think

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.634
I have in terms

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:08.568
of focusing

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:09.302
on the Republican Party

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:10.203
and ultimately focusing

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.338
on the opportunities for the country

00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:13.740
and having a little more of a candidate,

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.041
a little more unleashed voice

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.042
and getting folks

00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:17.877
to not just worry about, well,

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:19.412
is it a vote for Chris Sununu?

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.447
I'm not worried about that.

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:21.915
I want more independence

00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:22.982
on the Republican Party team.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:24.150
I want more young voters

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.785
on the Republican Party team.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:26.953
I'm tired of losing U.S.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.022
Senate races and governors races.

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:31.124
And clearly, I talk a bit candidly.

00:01:31.124 --> 00:01:32.392
I think as folks appreciate

00:01:32.392 --> 00:01:33.526
I think more folks

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:34.627
within the Republican Party

00:01:34.627 --> 00:01:34.994
have to have

00:01:34.994 --> 00:01:35.762
that kind of voice,

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:37.530
that kind of emphasis of message.

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:38.298
And making sure

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:40.633
that this is about the Republican Party,

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:42.669
not just about the former president,

00:01:42.669 --> 00:01:44.204
but part of your decision

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:46.039
is about the former president, right?

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:46.339
Is it?

00:01:46.339 --> 00:01:49.109
Oh, sure. About the size of the field.

00:01:49.242 --> 00:01:50.043
They're 12.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:50.777
By the end of the week,

00:01:50.777 --> 00:01:52.745
there will be 12 candidates

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:54.481
for the Republican nomination,

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:56.583
and you're not going to be one of them.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.885
But you think that's too big?

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.286
Well, it's look,

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:00.753
I don't mind

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:01.754
who gets into the field,

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.423
but given where the polls are right

00:02:03.423 --> 00:02:05.358
now, every candidate needs to understand

00:02:05.358 --> 00:02:06.726
the responsibility of getting out

00:02:06.726 --> 00:02:07.594
and getting out quickly

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.062
if it's not working.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:10.797
And I can be more candid about that

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:11.598
as the governor

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:12.499
of the first in the nation

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:13.399
primary, calling

00:02:13.399 --> 00:02:15.368
candidates, candidates out, saying, look,

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:16.236
you gave it a try,

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:17.470
you're still in low single digits.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.872
You've got to get out of the race.

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:19.806
And I'm not by when.

00:02:19.806 --> 00:02:21.774
What's the Christmas at the latest?

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.810
If we do what we did in 2016,

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:24.410
you're going to have

00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:25.879
somebody win this nomination

00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:27.914
with 35% of the Republican support.

00:02:28.281 --> 00:02:29.883
That's not where we should be as a party.

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.050
That's not where anybody wants to be.

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.285
But it's not just somebody for you.

00:02:32.285 --> 00:02:33.153
It's Donald Trump.

00:02:33.153 --> 00:02:33.586
Well it is.

00:02:33.586 --> 00:02:34.254
But again

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:36.222
it's somebody who is in the past

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:37.757
he he served the country.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:38.892
Thank you for your service.

00:02:38.892 --> 00:02:41.027
We have to be a party in a country

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.028
that goes forward.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.163
And if we're only talking

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:44.264
about Donald Trump,

00:02:44.264 --> 00:02:45.165
then we're only talking

00:02:45.165 --> 00:02:46.900
about relitigate elections

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:47.734
or January six.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.169
We're only talking about yesterday.

00:02:49.169 --> 00:02:50.837
But this is the United States of America.

00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:51.337
As a kid,

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:52.038
you got to be

00:02:52.038 --> 00:02:53.506
what you're going to bring to the table.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:54.574
How are you going to change things,

00:02:54.574 --> 00:02:55.241
what you're going to do?

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:56.709
And that's what gets people excited.

00:02:56.709 --> 00:02:58.077
Governor, you wrote an op

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:00.046
ed for The Washington Post, and in it

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:01.014
you said a few things

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:01.948
that I'm going to ask you about.

00:03:01.948 --> 00:03:02.348
But one,

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:04.384
just on this issue of the big field,

00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:07.620
you said the candidates should not

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.521
get into this race

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:11.591
to further a vanity campaign, sell books

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:12.358
or audition

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:14.260
to serve as Donald Trump's

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:15.461
vice president.

00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:16.062
Do you think that's

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:17.897
what some of them are? Absolutely.

00:03:17.897 --> 00:03:18.565
Oh, it's very fair.

00:03:18.565 --> 00:03:20.567
I mean, look, I don't want to

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:21.935
I don't want to be overly critical

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:23.536
until we see them on the debate stage.

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:24.671
Let's see who can take a punch.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:25.772
You can give a punch.

00:03:25.772 --> 00:03:27.307
But the message is very clear.

00:03:27.307 --> 00:03:28.942
If you're not talking in residents

00:03:28.942 --> 00:03:30.510
against the candidate who's right now

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.378
winning by 20 or 30 points,

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.414
then you're just potentially auditioning

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:35.448
to be on his team.

00:03:35.448 --> 00:03:36.482
So let's get them on that.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.951
If these candidates on the debate stage,

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:39.452
let's see who has the fire

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:41.354
to talk about the future of America

00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:43.356
and doesn't back down, who can actually,

00:03:43.590 --> 00:03:44.224
like I say, get

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:45.858
get a give a punch and take a punch,

00:03:45.858 --> 00:03:46.859
knows how to debate, knows

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:48.127
how to show show strength.

00:03:48.127 --> 00:03:49.762
And we'll see who those folks really are.

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:51.364
But clearly there's 12

00:03:51.364 --> 00:03:52.532
people in the race.

00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:54.667
I don't think all 12 of them

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:55.868
firmly believe that they can

00:03:55.868 --> 00:03:56.502
be president.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.770
I think a lot of a lot of them

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.672
just want to effectively audition

00:03:59.672 --> 00:04:01.241
to be in the cabinet or

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:03.476
or vice president at this time.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:04.644
There's no place for that.

00:04:04.644 --> 00:04:05.778
There's absolutely no place for that.

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:05.979
Well,

00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:09.449
you also on this subject, you say that

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:11.417
again that too many candidates

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:12.685
have entered this race.

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:13.386
They're simply running

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:14.887
to be Donald Trump's vice president.

00:04:14.887 --> 00:04:16.222
That's not leadership.

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:17.090
That's weak.

00:04:17.090 --> 00:04:17.824
It is weak.

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:18.691
But you want to be

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.393
you're not talking in generalities here.

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:21.394
But there are, again,

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:22.128
at the end of the week,

00:04:22.128 --> 00:04:22.962
12 candidates,

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.297
you have to be

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:25.898
thinking about somebody

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:27.367
or you're a candidate.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:28.334
I'll let everyone make

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:29.569
their own judgment today,

00:04:29.569 --> 00:04:31.904
but I'll be very verbal about that.

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:34.440
I have a suspicion Let's be fair.

00:04:34.474 --> 00:04:35.608
Let's let the process take place.

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:36.309
We're still a couple of months

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:37.010
away from the debate,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:38.578
so I don't want to be overly critical

00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:39.646
on any individual.

00:04:39.646 --> 00:04:40.680
But believe you me,

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.682
I will be very vocal for folks

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:44.050
that are just afraid

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:45.718
to take on the former president,

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:47.153
afraid to understand

00:04:47.153 --> 00:04:49.055
that the party is bigger than ourselves.

00:04:49.055 --> 00:04:50.323
This job of running

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:51.724
is bigger than yourself.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.493
It's not just about the vanity of it.

00:04:53.493 --> 00:04:55.194
It's not just to get on the stage and

00:04:55.194 --> 00:04:55.928
and sell books

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:57.096
or whatever it's going to be.

00:04:57.096 --> 00:04:58.665
It has to be about the party.

00:04:58.665 --> 00:04:59.932
It has to be about making sure

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:00.667
that the ticket

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:02.802
up and down is strong and right now.

00:05:02.802 --> 00:05:03.303
Donald Trump

00:05:03.303 --> 00:05:04.837
costs us from the US

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:05.905
Senate to governorships

00:05:05.905 --> 00:05:07.273
to school board seats.

00:05:07.273 --> 00:05:08.341
His message cost

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:09.175
Republican parties

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:10.176
dearly across the country.

00:05:10.176 --> 00:05:12.345
So if he is the nominee

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:16.049
will the Democrats win?

00:05:16.049 --> 00:05:17.917
Oh yeah. He can't win in November of 24.

00:05:17.917 --> 00:05:19.319
Oh, the math has shown

00:05:19.319 --> 00:05:21.187
Donald Trump has no chance

00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:22.655
of winning in November. Of 24.

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.324
He won't even win Georgia.

00:05:24.324 --> 00:05:25.158
If you're a Republican,

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:27.093
that can win Georgia of November 24,

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:27.960
you have no shot.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.662
And he's proven that. So.

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:30.797
And not only has he proven it,

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:31.364
but the candidate

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:33.066
he gets behind in a good conservative

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:34.867
state like Georgia lose the race.

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.469
His messaging doesn't translate.

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:39.205
It does well with a hard core 30 35% base

00:05:39.472 --> 00:05:41.407
but he loses everybody beyond there

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.109
and no one is undecided

00:05:43.109 --> 00:05:44.143
about the former president.

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:44.677
There's no

00:05:44.677 --> 00:05:45.478
there's no one out there going

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:47.480
well maybe I might consider voting

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:48.514
no you know where you are

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:49.682
he's a known commodity

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:51.150
and so the math doesn't add up

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.353
so if Republicans nominate him

00:05:53.553 --> 00:05:55.054
then we're seeing a vote for him

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:55.421
in the in

00:05:55.421 --> 00:05:56.522
the primary is effectively

00:05:56.522 --> 00:05:57.790
a vote for Joe Biden.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:58.725
I mean that's ultimately

00:05:58.725 --> 00:06:00.293
how the math will play out

00:06:00.293 --> 00:06:01.294
and you

00:06:01.294 --> 00:06:02.595
of course we're in New Hampshire

00:06:02.595 --> 00:06:04.630
and you are the governor of the first

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.366
in the nation primary state.

00:06:06.366 --> 00:06:08.134
Will you endorse a candidate?

00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:09.202
I suspect I might.

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:09.702
Yeah, I don't

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:10.503
I couldn't tell you who

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:11.070
right now

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:11.871
I mean, like I said,

00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.873
I think everyone has to see

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:14.607
when they go to those

00:06:14.607 --> 00:06:15.842
99 counties in Iowa,

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:17.110
when they sit in the living room

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:18.378
like this here in New Hampshire

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:20.413
and earn those votes we've seen

00:06:20.413 --> 00:06:21.180
in the past

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:22.281
the fall, you see

00:06:22.281 --> 00:06:23.583
candidates rise and fall.

00:06:23.583 --> 00:06:24.851
Some go, some come

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:25.785
who can hold their own.

00:06:25.785 --> 00:06:28.321
How do the debates Trump looks like now?

00:06:28.321 --> 00:06:29.422
Well, look, they're all friends.

00:06:29.422 --> 00:06:30.957
I mean, they really are.

00:06:30.957 --> 00:06:31.958
They're all good friends.

00:06:31.958 --> 00:06:34.026
And I hope they all stand up

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:34.861
and do the right thing

00:06:34.861 --> 00:06:35.995
campaign the right way.

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:36.963
Talk about America's

00:06:36.963 --> 00:06:38.698
opportunity in the future tense

00:06:38.698 --> 00:06:40.400
and not just capitulate to the past.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.634
Let's talk about a little bit more

00:06:41.634 --> 00:06:43.803
about your decision not to run

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:45.738
because you said the path again

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:46.606
in this Washington

00:06:46.606 --> 00:06:47.206
Post op ed,

00:06:47.206 --> 00:06:48.641
the path to winning was clear,

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:50.676
but I believe I can have a greater impact

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:51.611
influencing the future

00:06:51.611 --> 00:06:52.879
of the Republican Party

00:06:52.879 --> 00:06:54.046
and the nominating process.

00:06:54.046 --> 00:06:55.148
The beginning of that sentence,

00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:56.682
the path to winning was clear.

00:06:56.682 --> 00:06:58.151
If the path to winning was so clear,

00:06:58.151 --> 00:06:59.719
why aren't you on that path?

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:02.021
Well, so a couple a couple of reasons.

00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:05.224
When you start in as a candidate, again,

00:07:05.224 --> 00:07:06.359
it's vote for Kristen,

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:09.095
who get on my team, my team, my team.

00:07:09.095 --> 00:07:10.797
And so I think there's a responsibility

00:07:10.797 --> 00:07:11.764
to think a little bigger than that.

00:07:11.764 --> 00:07:12.899
It wouldn't have been easy.

00:07:12.899 --> 00:07:13.366
But, boy,

00:07:13.366 --> 00:07:15.168
when we pulled in a place like Iowa,

00:07:15.168 --> 00:07:16.169
you look at what?

00:07:16.169 --> 00:07:17.403
80% of Republicans,

00:07:17.403 --> 00:07:18.137
something like that, don't

00:07:18.137 --> 00:07:19.772
even participate in the caucus.

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:21.140
You got invited to the table

00:07:21.140 --> 00:07:22.275
they want to have a voice.

00:07:22.275 --> 00:07:23.342
They want a candidate

00:07:23.342 --> 00:07:24.877
that inspires them and get them excited.

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:25.745
So when we did

00:07:25.745 --> 00:07:26.579
all those numbers, while

00:07:26.579 --> 00:07:28.414
the math was really they're not easy,

00:07:28.414 --> 00:07:29.882
but a little unconventional,

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:31.851
but definitely a path and one I encourage

00:07:31.851 --> 00:07:32.685
all the other candidates

00:07:32.685 --> 00:07:33.753
to really look at.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:36.088
But I'm more concerned about the party

00:07:36.088 --> 00:07:36.923
as a whole.

00:07:36.923 --> 00:07:37.457
Look, I'm young.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:38.491
Maybe I could run in the future

00:07:38.491 --> 00:07:39.325
or something like that.

00:07:39.325 --> 00:07:40.560
But when I see what's happening

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:41.761
in all these other states,

00:07:41.761 --> 00:07:42.895
state legislatures, U.S.

00:07:42.895 --> 00:07:44.197
senators, governors,

00:07:44.197 --> 00:07:45.097
former President Trump

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:46.132
and his message,

00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:49.035
his style, his brand have cost us dearly.

00:07:49.035 --> 00:07:50.236
And he doesn't represent

00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:51.237
the Republican Party.

00:07:51.237 --> 00:07:52.371
He doesn't represent that

00:07:52.371 --> 00:07:53.206
limited government,

00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:55.007
local control, individual liberty, stuff

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:55.775
that we all talk about.

00:07:55.775 --> 00:07:57.009
He's he's about himself.

00:07:57.009 --> 00:07:58.277
And I just want Republicans

00:07:58.277 --> 00:07:59.645
to be about the Republican values

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:00.913
that have made a success.

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:01.581
Before I let you go,

00:08:01.581 --> 00:08:03.182
I want to push back on that,

00:08:03.182 --> 00:08:06.018
because if you look at the electorate,

00:08:06.018 --> 00:08:07.086
if you look at the candidates

00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:09.222
and what they're running on now,

00:08:09.222 --> 00:08:11.324
most of them, not all of them,

00:08:11.324 --> 00:08:13.259
they are running on issues

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:14.126
that are different

00:08:14.126 --> 00:08:15.761
from what you just described.

00:08:15.761 --> 00:08:16.429
One could argue

00:08:16.429 --> 00:08:17.530
that you're the one out of step

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:18.664
with the Republican Party,

00:08:18.664 --> 00:08:20.066
not the candidates. Running.

00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:20.833
Oh, no.

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:22.535
Look at Utah Republican Party.

00:08:22.535 --> 00:08:24.737
Well, maybe the base, right?

00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:25.538
Maybe the base is

00:08:25.538 --> 00:08:27.173
they're the ones who elect the nominee.

00:08:27.173 --> 00:08:27.607
That's right.

00:08:27.607 --> 00:08:28.641
And that's the frustration.

00:08:28.641 --> 00:08:30.109
So I want to make the base bigger.

00:08:30.109 --> 00:08:31.377
I want to get more independents

00:08:31.377 --> 00:08:31.911
into the base.

00:08:31.911 --> 00:08:32.912
I want more young people

00:08:32.912 --> 00:08:33.913
that have been Disenfranchized

00:08:33.913 --> 00:08:35.014
used to be part of the base,

00:08:35.014 --> 00:08:36.516
but we want to get them back in.

00:08:36.516 --> 00:08:37.817
You know, Gen Z, millennials

00:08:37.817 --> 00:08:39.719
will be a bigger part of the voting bloc

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:40.486
than ever before.

00:08:40.486 --> 00:08:41.387
And 24

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:42.121
if Republicans

00:08:42.121 --> 00:08:44.557
just see that we're losing it.

00:08:44.724 --> 00:08:46.292
So it's not just the Chris Sununu voice

00:08:46.292 --> 00:08:46.959
it has to bring,

00:08:46.959 --> 00:08:48.060
I think make the party bigger.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.295
I want more Republicans

00:08:49.295 --> 00:08:50.930
to stand up with this attitude of

00:08:50.930 --> 00:08:51.998
how do we get the team

00:08:51.998 --> 00:08:53.566
bigger, better and stronger.

00:08:53.566 --> 00:08:55.201
So that base that you're talking about

00:08:55.201 --> 00:08:56.102
isn't just stuck

00:08:56.102 --> 00:08:57.970
in a narrow version of yesterday

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.238
it really has to be about

00:08:59.238 --> 00:09:00.373
the bigger opportunity,

00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:01.641
more fiscal discipline.

00:09:01.641 --> 00:09:02.508
I mean, former President Trump

00:09:02.508 --> 00:09:04.510
added a trillion to the deficit.

00:09:04.710 --> 00:09:04.977
I mean,

00:09:05.244 --> 00:09:06.546
where did the Republicans

00:09:06.546 --> 00:09:07.780
go with fiscal discipline?

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:09.882
Or the opportunities on homelessness

00:09:09.882 --> 00:09:10.583
and the environment,

00:09:10.583 --> 00:09:12.852
conservative solutions to those things.

00:09:12.985 --> 00:09:14.253
We capitulate

00:09:14.253 --> 00:09:15.788
and hand it over to the Democrats

00:09:15.788 --> 00:09:17.189
to give kind of socialist

00:09:17.189 --> 00:09:18.024
green New Deal type

00:09:18.024 --> 00:09:19.792
solutions that have failed miserably,

00:09:19.792 --> 00:09:21.494
whereas we can bring real, real,

00:09:21.494 --> 00:09:22.895
I think, opportunity to the table

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:24.530
and that gets people excited.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:25.331
Governor, thank

00:09:25.331 --> 00:09:26.265
you so much for your time.

